ible that as I was suggesting before  the United Nations decided to go into  into Bosnia or NATO rather go into  Bosnia because so many people felt some  kind of guilt at having watched what  they watched and it being incapable of  doing anything about it so I think that  technology is going to play a very  important role in the way we respond to  future Wars yes were you tempted to look  at the Soviet Union's repression of  historical fact that seems to be the  most prominent almost in the century  yes I certainly could have and I and I  was tempted it was so huge that I felt  that in the context of one book I  couldn't include the Soviet Union I  would like to say that the the countries  that I've chosen and the subjects that  I've chosen to explore represent what I  have found to be archetypal ways that  nations deal with the past either by  denial either by fabricating some kind  of myth by trying to deal with it  through trials or whatever and can be  extrapolated to a number of other  countries I also chose places because of  the immensity of this project where I  had at least some beginnings of  knowledge about the subject in question  before I began but yes somebody should  write that book  yes iris what in your opinion was most  shocking revelation become out of here  three years of research was there  anything that may have surprised you  more than anything else as you set out  undertake this journey  I think what surprised me it wasn't  talking in a negative sense it was  surprising in a very positive sense was  how fiercely ordinary people will fight  to have their story told no matter how  it has been suppressed or for how many  years the TRC hearing that I attended in  loosey-goosey key concerned something  that had happened in 1961 between two  groups of black South Africans and they  had never been allowed to talk about it  before and they were officially talking  about it to the commissioners of the TRC  and it was for them an extraordinary  occasio